  "notes": "Eurodance crossed with American R&B vocal technique, and both halves of that fusion are authored. The R&B half is the harmony and the topline: the entry says the vamps carry \"R&B extensions — 7ths and 9ths rather than bare triads — because the topline is written for a genuine gospel/R&B singer rather than a hook shouter\", so `extensions` inverts the lane's triad-heavy default to 0.30/0.46/0.24, and \"melisma and real range on the sung part\" is the widest interval spread and highest note density in the 1990s block. The Eurodance half is the kick and the open hats. Between them sits the drum claim the entry marks `[LOW-CONF]` and this model repeats: \"the fusion with R&B shows as a lightly swung 16th hat and ghosted percussion rather than a rigid grid\" — 0.54 swing, the lowest swung setting on this axis, with the ghost probability up at 0.32. The bass has \"a walking, R&B-derived motion between chord tones\", which is an independent riff at 0.5 roots with the widest passing-tone set here. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are 0.54 itself, the ghost positions and velocities, and the melody register — \"real range\" is a direction, not a number.",
